`]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"Where a GPU keeps its data",children:[e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"The chip"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["An H100 SXM is one chip with 132 streaming multiprocessors (SMs), the units that run a kernel's threads and do its arithmetic ",e.jsx(a,{id:"nvidia-hopper-architecture-2026",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),". The figure redraws NVIDIA's diagram of the full GH100 die, which has 144 SMs in 8 groups called GPCs, each with 9 pairs of SMs called TPCs. The H100 SXM enables 132 of the 144. All SMs share one L2 cache of 50 MB (60 MB on the full die). The L2 is partitioned, and each partition caches data for the SMs in the GPCs connected to it ",e.jsx(a,{id:"nvidia-hopper-architecture-2026",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),". Off the chip, five stacks of HBM3 hold 80 GB, reached through memory controllers at the chip's edges ",e.jsx(a,{id:"nvidia-hopper-architecture-2026",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),", and deliver up to 3.35 TB/s ",e.jsx(a,{id:"nvidia-h100-2026",children:"NVIDIA, 2026a"}),". PCIe Gen5 connects the GPU to the host CPU at 64 GB/s in each direction. Eighteen NVLink links, 900 GB/s in total, connect it to other GPUs, the topic of Week 6 ",e.jsx(a,{id:"nvidia-hopper-architecture-2026",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),"."]}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"One SM"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Click an SM in the figure, or the SM button, to open one. Each SM is partitioned into four sub-partitions. A kernel's threads run in groups of 32 called warps, and each sub-partition has its own warp scheduler, which picks the next warp to run. Each sub-partition also has its own register file and execution units: integer and floating-point units, load/store units, a special-function unit and Tensor Cores ",e.jsx(a,{id:"nvidia2026nsight",children:"NVIDIA, 2026d"}),", one per sub-partition and four per SM ",e.jsx(a,{id:"nvidia-hopper-architecture-2026",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),". Tensor Cores are the GPU units that execute matrix multiplications. A sub-partition's register file holds 16,384 32-bit registers, 64 KB, so an SM has 256 KB of registers ",e.jsx(a,{id:"nvidia-hopper-architecture-2026",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),". The four sub-partitions share one 256 KB block of L1 data cache and shared memory, of which up to 228 KB can be shared memory, small on-chip storage for working data ",e.jsx(a,{id:"nvidia-hopper-architecture-2026",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"})," ",e.jsx(a,{id:"nvidia-hopper-tuning-2026",children:"NVIDIA, 2026c"}),". The instruction caches (L0 and L1) hold the kernel's instructions, and the texture units serve texture fetches. Neither holds tensor data."]}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"Four memory tiers"}),e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Tier"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Capacity on the H100 SXM"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Bandwidth"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Latency"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Registers"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"256 KB per SM"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"not given"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"a few ns"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Shared memory"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"up to 228 KB per SM"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"31 TB/s over all SMs, peak"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"about 10–20 ns"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"L2 cache"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"50 MB"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"12 TB/s, peak"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"about 150–200 ns"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"HBM3"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"80 GB"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"3.35 TB/s"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"about 400 ns"})]})]})]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The bandwidths of shared memory and L2 come from Table 1 of the FlashAttention-3 paper ",e.jsx(a,{id:"shah2024",children:"Shah et al., 2024"}),", and the latencies are the rounded figures that Guo et al. give for an H100 ",e.jsx(a,{id:"guo2025quack",children:"Guo et al., 2025"}),". The shared-memory figure is a peak, which Shah et al. derive from 128 bytes per clock cycle per SM, 132 SMs and a clock of 1,830 MHz ",e.jsx(a,{id:"shah2024",children:"Shah et al., 2024"}),": ",e.jsx(s,{math:"128 \\times 132 \\times 1.83 \\times 10^{9} \\approx 3.09 \\times 10^{13}",display:!1})," byte/s. Measured rates over the whole device fall below the peaks, about 20–30 TB/s for shared memory and 5–10 TB/s for L2 ",e.jsx(a,{id:"guo2025quack",children:"Guo et al., 2025"}),"."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Registers and shared memory keep data closest to the arithmetic. L2 can keep data for reuse across the blocks of threads that run on different SMs. Requests that none of these on-chip tiers serve go to HBM and count as HBM traffic."}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"Fast memory is small"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Compare the two ends of the table, in our own arithmetic. Shared memory has about ",e.jsx(s,{math:"31/3.35 \\approx 9",display:!1})," times the bandwidth of HBM and 20 to 40 times lower latency. All SMs together hold ",e.jsx(s,{math:"132 \\times 228\\ \\text{KB} \\approx 30\\ \\text{MB}",display:!1})," of it, about 2,700 times less than the 80 GB of HBM. Capacity says whether a working set fits in a tier, and bandwidth says how fast that tier serves transfers. A model's tensors take gigabytes, and nothing on the chip survives from one kernel to the next. Every kernel therefore reads its inputs from HBM and writes its outputs back to it, through the slowest tier, and those bytes are the first that the next slide's bound counts. The lever is reuse: bring a block of data on chip once and use it many times while it is there. FlashAttention's tiling pulls this lever."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The FlashAttention papers call the memory inside each SM, its registers and shared memory, SRAM (static RAM). The L2 cache is on-chip SRAM too, but their model of the GPU has two levels only, HBM and SRAM ",e.jsx(a,{id:"dao2022",children:"Dao et al., 2022"}),"."]})]})]}),e.jsx(c,{caption:"The SM view: four sub-partitions, each with its own register file, share one 256 KB block of L1 data cache and shared memory. Registers and shared memory are the on-chip SRAM, small and fast, that the FlashAttention tab keeps its blocks in.",children:e.jsx(u,{view:"sm",focus:"memory",source:e.jsxs(e.Fragment,{children:["After ",e.jsx(a,{id:"nvidia-hopper-architecture-2026",paren:!1,children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),", Figures 3 and 4"]})})}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Every kernel computes and moves bytes",accent:2,number:"8",children:[e.jsx(s,{math:"T \\ge \\max\\left(\\frac{F}{P_{\\mathrm{peak}}},\\ \\frac{B}{\\beta}\\right)",label:"resource lower bound"}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Compute time: FLOPs ÷ peak"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Movement time: bytes ÷ bandwidth"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"The slower one binds"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"A lower bound on time from two resources",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A GPU kernel spends time on two things: the arithmetic it performs, and the data it moves between memory and the units that compute. Each has a rate limit."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Write ",e.jsx(s,{math:"F",display:!1})," for the useful arithmetic in floating-point operations (FLOPs) and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"P_{\\mathrm{peak}}",display:!1})," for the device's peak arithmetic rate in FLOP/s. Write ",e.jsx(s,{math:"B",display:!1})," for the bytes that cross the boundary of one chosen memory tier and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\beta",display:!1})," for that tier's bandwidth in bytes per second. In this lecture's formulas ",e.jsx(s,{math:"B",display:!1})," counts bytes, and the batch size is written out in words. In a tensor shape such as ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[B,H,S,S]"}),", ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"B"})," still names the batch axis."]}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"A1. Two rate limits."})," The kernel performs ",e.jsx(s,{math:"F",display:!1})," FLOPs at a rate of at most ",e.jsx(s,{math:"P_{\\mathrm{peak}}",display:!1}),", and it moves ",e.jsx(s,{math:"B",display:!1})," bytes across the chosen tier at a rate of at most ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\beta",display:!1}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"A2. Ideal overlap."})," Arithmetic and movement run fully at the same time, so neither waits for the other."]}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Claim (lower bound)."})," Under A1, the kernel's time ",e.jsx(s,{math:"T",display:!1}),", in seconds, satisfies"]}),e.jsx(s,{math:"T \\ge \\max\\left(\\frac{F}{P_{\\mathrm{peak}}},\\ \\frac{B}{\\beta}\\right)."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The arithmetic alone takes at least ",e.jsx(s,{math:"F/P_{\\mathrm{peak}}",display:!1})," and the movement alone at least ",e.jsx(s,{math:"B/\\beta",display:!1}),", so ",e.jsx(s,{math:"T",display:!1})," is at least the larger of the two."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Both bounds hold whatever the overlap, so the expression is a lower bound, not a prediction of the latency. A kernel reaches it only under A2. In a real kernel arithmetic and movement overlap only in part, or they add up, and the time lies above the bound. Launch, dependency and synchronization costs are outside the expression altogether."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:['The slower resource binds: making the other one faster does not lower the floor. Which resource binds depends on the workload (its shape, its data type and how it is implemented) and on which memory tier you count. "Memory-bound" therefore describes a stated workload at a stated memory tier, and the name of an operation does not settle it. Use the comparison to decide what to inspect, then measure ',e.jsx(s,{math:"F",display:!1}),", the traffic and the elapsed time over the same region of code, on the system you run."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"memory wall"})," is the name for a trend: the compute available on a device grows faster than the rate at which memory can supply its data. Roofline analysis turns the comparison above into a calculation for one device."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Arithmetic intensity counts FLOPs per byte",accent:3,number:"9",children:[e.jsx(s,{math:"I = \\frac{F_{\\mathrm{useful}}}{B_{\\mathrm{HBM}}}",label:"arithmetic intensity"}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
