`]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"Three ways DeepSeek shrank the cache",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Figure 1b of the DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash report plots the global KV cache of four DeepSeek generations in bytes per token, summed over all layers that keep one ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2026deepseekv41",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026b"}),". The four bars share one linear axis: DeepSeek-V1 (November 2023) at 389,120 bytes, DeepSeek-V3.2 (December 2025) at 48,068, DeepSeek-V4-Flash (April 2026) at 3,514 and DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash (September 2026) at 890. From the first bar to the last the factor is ",e.jsx(s,{math:"389{,}120 / 890 \\approx 437.2",display:!1}),", which the report's caption rounds to 437-fold ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2026deepseekv41",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026b"}),"."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The four bars are four different models, each in its own deployed storage format. They differ in size, depth and much else, so the figure is no controlled ablation, and no single step isolates one mechanism. Between the bars, three mechanisms changed what is stored. V3.2 stores a narrow MLA entry, with the latent in FP8, in place of V1's per-head keys and values. V4-Flash keeps fewer entries along the sequence, because CSA and HCA merge tokens. V4.1-Flash keeps fewer entries across layers, because most of its layers reuse the entries of an earlier layer."}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"Fewer entries along the sequence: CSA and HCA"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["DeepSeek-V4, the next model after V3.2, keeps DSA's selection and also reduces the number of entries along the sequence ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekv4",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026a"}),". In Compressed Sparse Attention (CSA), a trained compressor merges the key-value entries of every 4 tokens into one entry, and DSA's lightning indexer and top-k select among these compressed entries. Heavily Compressed Attention (HCA) merges every 128 tokens into one entry and attends to every earlier entry, with no indexer. CSA and HCA layers are interleaved. Both add an uncompressed sliding window over the most recent 128 tokens, because a query attends only to earlier compressed entries and could not otherwise reach the earlier tokens of its own, not yet compressed group ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekv4",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026a"}),". In terms of stored state and entries read, compression changes what the cache stores, and CSA's top-k changes which stored entries each query reads."]}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"Fewer entries across layers: CSA2"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash replaces V4's mix of CSA and HCA with Compressed Sparse Attention 2 (CSA2) alone, which reuses the main KV entries, the indexer keys and the top-k indices across layers ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2026deepseekv41",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026b"}),". A Full layer computes and stores new entries and selects a fresh top-k. A Reindex layer reuses a preceding layer's entries and indexer keys and selects its own top-k. A Reuse layer reuses both the entries and the top-k indices. Of V4.1-Flash's 40 layers, the first two use only the sliding window and keep no global entries. The other 38 are 4 Full, 4 Reindex and 30 Reuse layers, so 34 layers reuse the entries of 4 ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2026deepseekv41",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026b"}),". V4.1-Flash also stores its main KV entries in FP4, a 4-bit format with one 8-bit scale for every 16 values, while its sliding-window cache stays in FP8 ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2026deepseekv41",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026b"}),"."]}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"What the figure leaves out"}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:'"Global" leaves two caches out: the sliding-window cache that V4 and V4.1 keep beside the compressed entries, and the cache of any multi-token prediction layers.'})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(h,{tab:"Summary",plate:"zonca-boat-lift",children:[e.jsxs(i,{title:"What limits attention, and how models cut it",accent:1,number:"48",items:"5",children:[e.jsxs(t.ol,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Roofline finds what binds, MFU compares with a matching peak"}),`
