`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"the kernel work, spent on computing attention."}),`
`]}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"Which resource binds"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The roofline compares an operation's arithmetic intensity ",e.jsx(s,{math:"I = F_{\\mathrm{useful}}/B_{\\mathrm{HBM}}",display:!1}),", its useful FLOPs per byte of HBM traffic under a stated traffic model, with the ridge point ",e.jsx(s,{math:"I_{\\mathrm{ridge}} = P_{\\mathrm{peak}}/\\beta",display:!1}),". Below the ridge bandwidth binds, and above it compute can. Counting only its score-matrix traffic, naive attention at the lecture's example shape reached about 85 FLOP per byte, against an H100 SXM ridge of about 295. Single-device MFU divides the model's FLOP/s by one peak that matches the run's precision and its dense or sparse convention, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\mathrm{MFU}_{1\\mathrm{GPU}} = P_{\\mathrm{model}}/P_{\\mathrm{peak,matching}}",display:!1}),"."]}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"FlashAttention moves fewer bytes"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["FlashAttention computes the same function as dense attention. Tiling, online softmax and recomputation keep the score matrix out of HBM, at the price of extra arithmetic in the backward pass. For the attention of GPT-2 medium on an A100 at sequence length 1,024, forward and backward pass together, it did 13% more arithmetic and ran three times faster ",e.jsx(a,{id:"dao2022",children:"Dao et al., 2022"}),", because the traffic it avoided cost more time than the arithmetic it added."]}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"A window gives up reach"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A local layer reads only the ",e.jsx(s,{math:"w",display:!1})," most recent positions, and depth widens the reach only linearly, to ",e.jsx(s,{math:"R_l = 1 + l(w-1)",display:!1})," positions after ",e.jsx(s,{math:"l",display:!1})," layers, capped by the prefix. The mask changes which keys are visible, which is semantic sparsity. The work falls to ",e.jsx(s,{math:"O(Sw)",display:!1})," only with a kernel that skips the masked keys, which is execution sparsity, and the decode cache falls to ",e.jsx(s,{math:"O(w)",display:!1})," only with eviction that matches the window."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Evicting the first positions from a rolling window made perplexity much worse, and keeping a few restored it. In Xiao et al.'s models these positions are attention sinks, on which some trained heads put much of their weight ",e.jsx(a,{id:"xiao2024",children:"Xiao et al., 2024"}),". The recovery is observed, and the softmax explanation is a hypothesis. A retained sink is a real position in the cache, while gpt-oss's learned sink bias is a per-head term in the softmax denominator with no token behind it ",e.jsx(a,{id:"gptoss2025",children:"OpenAI, 2025"}),"."]}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"Storing less and reading less"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["MLA stores one shared content latent and one shared rotary key per token and pays in reconstruction: in the unabsorbed form each head rebuilds its content key and value at every use. Which form stores less depends on the configuration. With 16 query heads, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"n_{kv} = 2",display:!1}),", ",e.jsx(s,{math:"d_h = 128",display:!1}),", ",e.jsx(s,{math:"d_c = 512",display:!1})," and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"d_r = 64",display:!1}),", MHA stores 4,096 elements per token per layer, GQA 512 and MLA 576, so here GQA stores slightly fewer than MLA. DSA, which selects tokens, and MSA, which selects blocks, leave the stored cache as it is and change which entries each query reads. They pay in index work and in recall, because a token the indexer does not select cannot influence the query."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Modern models combine these mechanisms, and how a combination performs is a measurement of one setup. Measure the bottleneck of your workload before you choose, because no mechanism wins by its name."}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"Check yourself"}),e.jsxs(t.ol,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"What is the difference between semantic and execution sparsity?"}),`
