`]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Work on the reflection questions without hints, and bring your reasoning to the tutorial. Week 5 turns from what attention costs on a GPU to how a model is trained: optimizers, learning-rate schedules, and numerical stability."})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Choose the mechanism that changes the bottleneck",reading:!0,accent:6,number:"49",children:[e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"condition"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"response"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"If exact dense semantics are required and HBM traffic binds,"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"use an IO-aware dense kernel."})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"If bounded streaming memory and local semantics are acceptable,"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"pair SWA with matching eviction and test retained sinks separately."})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"If dense reach must remain while per-token stored state falls,"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"compare GQA and unabsorbed MLA, then measure decode."})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"If long reach is required but attending every stored token is too costly,"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"evaluate learned selection recall, gather bytes, and synchronized latency."})]})]})]}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Attribute each change to its mechanism"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"Each condition points to one mechanism",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Each row names a condition of the workload and the mechanism that changes its bottleneck. If exact dense attention is required while HBM traffic binds, an IO-aware dense kernel such as FlashAttention computes the same function with fewer HBM reads and writes. If the cache must stay within a fixed size while text streams in, and nearby tokens suffice, pair the sliding window with matching eviction, and test retained sinks separately, because they change perplexity and cost cache bytes of their own. If dense reach must stay while the state stored per token falls, compare GQA with unabsorbed MLA and measure decoding, where every step reads the cache. If long reach is needed and attending to every stored token costs too much, evaluate learned selection on its recall, the bytes it gathers and its synchronized latency."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"When mechanisms are combined, attribute each measured change to the mechanism that caused it. GQA and a window with matching eviction both shrink the decode cache, for example, so in a model that uses both, a smaller cache could come from either."})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"What these mechanisms leave open",reading:!0,accent:0,number:"50",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
