`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"The roofline. Each point gets its arithmetic intensity, the achieved model FLOP/s, the achieved bandwidth and the single-device MFU, all under one named convention for traffic, precision, timing region and dense or sparse peak. The FLOP count is model FLOPs: its attention term counts only the query-key pairs the mask leaves valid. The record also gives the whole square that a kernel multiplies when it cannot skip masked pairs, so a windowed point on such a kernel shows the work it does without credit for it."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Context for the points. A dense arm is forwarded without a mask, so the backend applies causality itself and can skip the masked half of the square. A windowed arm needs the band as an explicit mask. One large BF16 matrix multiplication shows what the device reaches at all. The fastest multi-head point runs once more under ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"torch.compile"}),", which fuses the elementwise work between the matrix multiplications. A decode step, one new query per sequence against a cache, is timed for each attention form at three cache lengths, because decoding is where the stored cache is read."]}),`
`]}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"The window-tradeoff run"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The window-tradeoff run is a provided script, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"exercises/week-04/window_tradeoff.py"}),", which you run but do not write. Every SDPA backend multiplies the whole square of an explicit mask, so the window saves no work on the protocol's windowed points. This run shows where it can. It times attention alone in three ways: under SDPA with the window's band, pinned to cuDNN, and under compiled FlexAttention twice, once with a causal block mask and once with a block mask built from your ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"window_mask_mod"}),". The windowed block mask lets the kernel skip the tiles outside the window. The run also counts, as arithmetic, the positions an eight-layer stack would keep cached if each local layer evicted what its window no longer reads, for a dense, an all-local and an interleaved stack. Finally it reads the supplied trio, three models that the course staff trained at one budget and that differ only in reach: dense, all-local and interleaved. Their results are reported as held-out perplexity and as mean loss over buckets of positions."]})]})]}),e.jsx(i,{title:"To-dos until next week",accent:5,number:"53",children:e.jsxs("div",{className:"flex flex-col gap-10",children:[e.jsx("div",{className:"flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-x-24 gap-y-6",children:e.jsxs("figure",{className:"text-center",children:[e.jsx("a",{href:"https://tally.so/r/e55djo",children:e.jsx("img",{src:"images/qr-week-04-muddiest-point.svg",alt:"QR code for the muddiest-point survey, tally.so/r/e55djo",className:"w-56 h-56"})}),e.jsxs("figcaption",{children:[e.jsx("strong",{children:"Muddiest point"}),e.jsx("br",{}),"Do it right now! (2 min)"]})]})}),e.jsxs("div",{className:"grid gap-x-12 gap-y-6 md:grid-cols-3",children:[e.jsxs("div",{children:[e.jsx("div",{className:"font-semibold",children:"Before the tutorial"}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
